  "title": "Contiguous Array",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Prefix sum",
  "patterns": [
    "prefix sum",
    "hash map"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "if you relabel every 0 as -1, a subarray with equal 0s and 1s becomes a subarray that sums to exactly 0 - which earlier idea does that resemble?",
    "a running sum returning to the same value twice means the elements between those two points summed to zero",
    "keep a hash map from running-sum-value to the FIRST index where that sum occurred (seed it with sum 0 at index -1)",
    "at each index, if the current running sum has been seen before, the subarray between that earlier index and now is balanced - track the longest such gap"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "storing every occurrence of a sum instead of only the first (later occurrences give shorter subarrays)",
    "forgetting to seed the map with sum 0 at index -1, which misses subarrays balanced from the very start",
    "not relabeling 0 as -1 and instead trying to track counts of 0s and 1s separately, which is much messier"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a binary array `nums`, containing only 0s and 1s.\n\n**Return the length of the longest contiguous subarray of `nums` that contains an equal number of 0s and 1s.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^5",
      "nums[i] is either 0 or 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "no balanced subarray longer than 0 exists",
      "the entire array is already balanced",
      "the array has a single element (never balanced)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
